WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.102 --> 00:00:05.739
NARRATOR: 40 miles off the
coast of South Australia, shark

00:00:05.805 --> 00:00:09.442
scientist Doctor Charlie
Huveneers and PhD student

00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:11.945
Madeline Riley
investigate the best

00:00:12.011 --> 00:00:14.347
way to fend off a shark attack.

00:00:18.051 --> 00:00:21.087
The answer may be to prevent
it in the first place.

00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:25.558
And then we just
need a few base ready.

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:26.559
And you're good to go.

00:00:26.626 --> 00:00:27.527
All right, sounds good.

00:00:27.594 --> 00:00:28.862
Let's hope the
sharks stick around.

00:00:28.928 --> 00:00:31.564
You first!

00:00:31.631 --> 00:00:34.868
NARRATOR: Their theory, that
the sharks ampulae of Lorenzini

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:36.503
can be used against it.

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:39.072
[music playing]

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:51.651
They're testing a new shark
deterrent that does just that.

00:00:51.718 --> 00:00:53.153
So sharks are
capable of detecting

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:54.621
minute electric fields.

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:56.890
And the idea of this
electric deterrent

00:00:56.956 --> 00:00:59.692
is to transmit a very
strong electric field that

00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:01.861
will overwhelm that
sense of the shark,

00:01:01.928 --> 00:01:04.063
so that as the shark
gets too close,

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:06.666
it's kind of nearly receiving
a punch in the face.

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:09.068
And the shark just
basically deterred.

00:01:09.135 --> 00:01:10.970
NARRATOR: There's a lot
riding on this test.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:15.475
Most shark deterrents
are designed

00:01:15.542 --> 00:01:17.043
to protect just one person.

00:01:19.879 --> 00:01:22.916
This one, which has been
tested for two years,

00:01:22.982 --> 00:01:24.350
is much more ambitious.

00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:26.586
[music playing]

00:01:28.822 --> 00:01:32.826
If all goes well, it'll be
powerful enough to keep sharks

00:01:32.892 --> 00:01:35.328
out of a section of
the ocean 25 feet

00:01:35.395 --> 00:01:41.534
in diameter by 50 feet deep
without harming any swimmers.

00:01:41.601 --> 00:01:43.369
That's bigger than
most swimming pools.

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:48.708
How it works is this big,
white thing is a float.

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:50.643
It just floats on
top of the water.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:53.880
This black module is where
the power comes from.

00:01:53.947 --> 00:01:58.084
And then underneath, we've got
a 4-meter long electrode, which

00:01:58.151 --> 00:01:59.853
actually emits the
electric field, which

00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:01.454
theoretically deters a shark.

00:02:01.521 --> 00:02:03.289
[music playing]

00:02:20.607 --> 00:02:22.108
NARRATOR: The first
thing they'll need

00:02:22.175 --> 00:02:26.880
to test the deterrent, sharks.

00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:31.117
Within moments, a 13-foot
great white turns up.

00:02:31.184 --> 00:02:33.920
All right, we've got a
shark coming in at the back.

00:02:33.987 --> 00:02:38.658
NARRATOR: And seems
curious about the device.

00:02:38.725 --> 00:02:43.329
A large piece of bait
hangs near the electrode.

00:02:43.396 --> 00:02:45.365
If the shark's
hungry, it'll have

00:02:45.431 --> 00:02:48.001
to fight through the
electric field to get to it.

00:02:51.037 --> 00:02:55.174
The team will film the shark's
reaction then analyze it after.

00:02:59.345 --> 00:03:03.750
The shark circles, clearly
intrigued by the tasty morsel

00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:06.452
floating in the ocean.

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:09.222
Coming back around.

00:03:09.289 --> 00:03:11.224
NARRATOR: Scientists
monitor the action

00:03:11.291 --> 00:03:13.760
as best they can from the deck.

00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:18.264
They know that the entire
test is riding on this moment.

00:03:18.331 --> 00:03:21.067
Investigators wait to
see if the great white

00:03:21.134 --> 00:03:23.636
will break through the
constant electric current.

00:03:27.974 --> 00:03:43.656
The shark gets closer
and closer with each pass

00:03:43.723 --> 00:03:44.824
but doesn't bite.

00:03:44.891 --> 00:03:47.393
[music playing]

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:57.537
The team reviews the footage,
looking for the telltale muscle

00:03:57.604 --> 00:04:01.374
spasm that signals that the
electrical current affected

00:04:01.441 --> 00:04:03.710
the shark.

00:04:03.776 --> 00:04:04.677
Oh, did you see that?

00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:06.179
Oh, you can
actually literally see

00:04:06.245 --> 00:04:07.880
the pectoral fin flicking down.

00:04:07.947 --> 00:04:09.449
Yeah.

00:04:09.515 --> 00:04:10.950
NARRATOR: It's a
subtle movement,

00:04:11.017 --> 00:04:13.953
but the slight flick of
the shark's pectoral fin

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:18.558
tells the research team the
deterrent seems to work.

00:04:18.625 --> 00:04:20.860
That first flexing
of the pectoral fin,

00:04:20.927 --> 00:04:23.096
that's a standard reaction
to an electric field

00:04:23.162 --> 00:04:24.430
that we've seen in the past.

00:04:24.497 --> 00:04:25.398
Yeah.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:30.169
We're in the really early
stages of this testing.

00:04:30.236 --> 00:04:33.039
But if we get more really good
trials like we've had today,

00:04:33.106 --> 00:04:36.309
then we'll get a clearer
understanding of how effective

00:04:36.376 --> 00:04:37.910
this deterrent actually is.

00:04:37.977 --> 00:04:40.713
[music playing]

